## Artifact durability (apply only to `kind: done` signals)

This check applies **only** when the proposed entry is a `kind: done` signal recording completed work. For any other entry — decisions, non-done signals — emit no finding from this section.

A done signal's referenced artifacts must be durable in the Git-tracked repository. Accept any of: a prior commit referenced by the entry, attachments on this entry (`--attach`), or attachments on a referenced upstream entry already in the graph.

- `high` — A `kind: done` signal claims work was done but references no durable artifact (no commit, no attachment, no upstream attachment). The description alone is not evidence of durability.
- `low` — Durable artifacts exist but the entry doesn't explicitly name them (e.g. describes changes without citing the commit or attachment).
- No finding — The proposed entry is not a `kind: done` signal; or it references a commit, carries an attachment, or references an upstream entry that has the relevant attachment.
